---
import Code from "../../../../components/Code.astro"
import readme from "../../../../../packages/opui/README.md?raw"

type Props = {
  tools: ("next" | "vite")[]
}

const { tools } = Astro.props

const SECTION = "### Builds that rewrite `light-dark()`"
const start = readme.indexOf(SECTION)
const section = readme.slice(start, readme.indexOf("\n## ", start))
const blocks = new Map(
  [...section.matchAll(/```js\n(\/\/ (\w+)\.config[^\n]*\n[\s\S]*?)```/g)].map(
    ([, code = "", tool = ""]) => [tool, code.trim()],
  ),
)
const missing = tools.filter((tool) => !blocks.has(tool))
if (start === -1 || missing.length > 0) {
  throw new Error(
    `The README's "${SECTION}" section has no ${missing.join(" or ")}.config block`,
  )
}
---

<p>
  Vite 8, which SvelteKit and Nuxt build with, and Next 16 rewrite
  <code>light-dark()</code> for older browsers by default. The colors still
  follow the OS, and <code>.ui-light</code> or <code>.ui-dark</code> on
  <code>html</code> still works, but on a part of the page it stops switching
  after a build. Target browsers that have <code>light-dark()</code>:
</p>
{tools.map((tool) => (
  <Code code={blocks.get(tool)!} lang="js" />
))}
<p>
  Vite also prints warnings like
  <code>'scroll-button' is not recognized as a valid pseudo-element</code>
  for the Carousel, even without one on the page. They're harmless: the rules
  stay in the output.
</p>
